t":"பசி","lemma":"பசி","gloss":"hunger"},{"text":"த்து","lemma":"த்து","gloss":"verbal participle"}],"gloss":"having hungered"},{"surface":"பணை","parts":[{"text":"பணை","lemma":"பணை","gloss":"sugar cane field"}],"gloss":"sugar cane field"},{"surface":"முயலும்","parts":[{"text":"முயல்","lemma":"முயல்","gloss":"strive"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"உம்","gloss":"adjectival participle"}],"gloss":"striving"},{"surface":"யானை","parts":[{"text":"யானை","lemma":"யானை","gloss":"elephant"}],"gloss":"elephant"},{"surface":"போல","parts":[{"text":"போல்","lemma":"போல்","gloss":"resemble"},{"text":"அ","lemma":"அ","gloss":"infinitive"}],"gloss":"like"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"இரு","parts":[{"text":"இரு","lemma":"இரு","gloss":"two"}],"gloss":"two"},{"surface":"தலை","parts":[{"text":"தலை","lemma":"தலை","gloss":"side"}],"gloss":"sides"},{"surface":"ஒசிய","parts":[{"text":"ஒசி","lemma":"ஒசி","gloss":"bend"},{"text":"அ","lemma":"அ","gloss":"infinitive"}],"gloss":"to bend"},{"surface":"எற்றி","parts":[{"text":"எற்று","lemma":"எற்று","gloss":"strike"},{"text":"இ","lemma":"இ","gloss":"having"}],"gloss":"having struck"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"களம்","parts":[{"text":"களம்","lemma":"களம்","gloss":"arena"}],"gloss":"arena"},{"surface":"புகும்","parts":[{"text":"புகு","lemma":"புகு","gloss":"enter"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"உம்","gloss":"adjectival participle"}],"gloss":"entering"},{"surface":"மல்லன்","parts":[{"text":"மல்லன்","lemma":"மல்லன்","gloss":"wrestler"}],"gloss":"wrestler"},{"surface":"கடந்து","parts":[{"text":"கட","lemma":"கட","gloss":"overcome"},{"text":"ந்து","lemma":"ந்து","gloss":"having"}],"gloss":"having overcome"},{"surface":"அடு","parts":[{"text":"அடு","lemma":"அடு","gloss":"conquer"}],"gloss":"conquering"},{"surface":"நிலையே","parts":[{"text":"நிலை","lemma":"நிலை","gloss":"state"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"emphasis"}],"gloss":"is the state indeed"}]}],"literal":"Sweet, strong toddy's Āmūr in that place, / strong, mighty wrestler's furious strength having crushed, / at one time, chest retreats indeed; at another time, / attacking army line having withstood, back steps back indeed. / Whether he rewards or does not reward, victorious battle's / difficult battle's Tittan, may he see indeed, behold! / Hungry, sugar cane field striving elephant like, / two sides to bend, having struck, / arena entering wrestler's overcoming and conquering state indeed!","translation":"In Āmūr, a place of sweet and strong toddy, he crushed the furious strength of the mighty wrestler. At one moment, his chest retreats; at another moment, withstanding the attacking army line, he steps back. Whether Tittan of victorious battles rewards him or not, may he behold this! Like a hungry elephant that enters a sugar cane field and strikes it so that both sides bend, this is the state of the wrestler who enters the arena, overcoming and conquering.","modern":"இனிய, காரமான கள்ளையுடைய ஆமூர் என்னும் இடத்தில், வலிமைமிக்க மல்லனின் மத வலிமையை அழித்து, ஒருமுறை மார்பு ஒடுங்குகிறது; மற்றொருமுறை பகைவரின் படையைத் தாங்கிப் பின்வாங்கி நிற்கிறது. வெற்றி தரும் போர்களைச் செய்யும் தித்தன், பரிசில் தந்தாலும் தராவிட்டாலும், இதனைப் பார்ப்பானாக! பசியோடு கரும்புத் தோட்டத்தில் புகுந்து இருபுறமும் ஒசியுமாறு தாக்கும் யானையைப் போல, போர்க்களத்தில் புகுந்து பகைவரை வென்று கொல்லும் மல்லனின் நிலை இதுவே ஆகும்.","explanation":"The poet Sāttantaiyār sings in praise of the Chola prince Pōrvaikkō Perunaṟkiḷḷi, who fought a wrestling duel in the town of Āmūr. The tiṇai is tumpai (battle), and the tuṟai is erumai maṟam (the valor of a warrior who stands his ground like a wild buffalo). The poet describes the intense wrestling match where the prince first retreats slightly to absorb the opponent's blow and then steps back to withstand the charge. He compares the prince to a hungry elephant that enters a sugarcane field, crushing and bending the stalks on both sides. The poet wishes that the great king Tittan (the prince's father, with whom he had a strained relationship) would witness this glorious victory, regardless of whether he rewards the prince or not. Key words include 'mallaṉ' (wrestler), 'kaṭantu' (overcoming), and 'yāṉai' (elephant).","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}